{"The little velociraptor opened her jaws and hissed at Grant, in a posture of sudden intense fury.\n“Fascinating,” Grant said.\n“Can I stay and play with her?” Tim said.\n“Not right now,” Ed Regis said, glancing at his watch. “\003It’s three o’clock\004, and it’s a good time for a tour of the park itself, so you can see all the dinosaurs in the habitats we have designed for them.”","Jurassic Park","Michael Crichton",true},
{"\003Three o’clock\004 is always too late or too early for anything you want to do. An odd moment in the afternoon.","Nausea","Jean-Paul Sartre",true},
{"A few moments later, about \003three o’clock\004, Courfeyrec chanced to be passing along the Rue Mouffetard in company with Bossuet. The snow had redoubled in violence, and filled the air.","Les Misérables","Victor Hugo",true},
{"At \003three o’clock on the afternoon\004 there was a loud peal at the bell, an authoritative voice in the hall, and, to my surprise, no less a person than Mr. Athelney Jones was shown up to me. Very different was he, however, from the brusque and masterful professor of common sense who had taken over the case so confidently at Upper Norwood. His expression was downcast, and his bearing meek and even apologetic.","The Sign of Four","Sir Arthur Conan Doyle",true},
{"“Then good-night, your majesty, and I trust that we shall soon have some good news for you. And good-night, Watson,” he added, as the wheels of the royal brougham rolled down the street. “If you will be good enough to call to-morrow afternoon at \003three o’clock\004 I should like to chat this little matter over with you.”","The Sign of Four","Sir Arthur Conan Doyle",true},
{"The next afternoon at \003three\004, I went to meet the new teacher. When I stepped inside Julian’s office I was shocked. It was completely empty. The books, the rugs, the big round table were all gone.","The Secret History","Donna Tartt",true},
{"At about \003three o’clock in the afternoon\004, my whole body is hit by an overwhelming tiredness and I spent the last couple of hours of the day perusing the gift shop for stuffed koalas for Kay and Olivia and chilling out at a table inside the air-conditioned café.","Pictures of Lily","Paige Toon",true},
{"All the doors were locked, everything in its proper place. Nothing out of the ordinary. I went back to the janitor’s room, set my alarm for \003three\004, and fell fast asleep.","Blind Willow, Sleeping Woman","Haruki Murakami",true},
{"When the alarm went off at \003three\004, though, I woke up feeling weird. I can’t explain it, but I just felt different. I didn’t feel like getting up—it was like something was suppressing my will to get out of bed. I’m the type who usually leaps right out of bed, so I couldn’t understand it.","Blind Willow, Sleeping Woman","Haruki Murakami",true},
{"Luisa crosses the office to Dom Grelsch’s door. Her boss is speaking on the phone in a low, irate voice. Luisa waits outside but overhears. “No—no, no, Mr, Frum, it \001is\002 black-and-white, tell me—hey, \001I’m\002 talking now—tell me a \001more\002 black-and-white ‘condition’ than leukemia? Know what I think? I think my wife is just one piece of paperwork between you and your \003three o’clock\004 golf slot, isn’t she?”","Cloud Atlas","David Mitchell",true},
{"Jarvis whistled through his teeth. “Will the law help a man of your years bounce back from multiple spinal fractures, Timothy?”\nEddie: “Men of your age don’t bounce. They splat.”\nI fought with all my might, but my sphincter was no longer my own and a cannonade fired off. Amusement or condescension I could have borne, but my tormentors’ pity signified my abject defeat. The toilet chain was pulled.\n“\003Three o’clock\004,” Cavendish-Redux went down the pan.","Cloud Atlas","David Mitchell",false},
{"I had pushed too hard. “Damn it to hell, Tim, my bank \001crashed\002! We were bled dry by those bloodsuckers at Lloyds! The days when I had that kind of spondulics at my beck and call are gone, gone, gone! Out house is mortgaged, twice over! I’m the mighty fallen, you’re the miniscule fallen. Anyway, you’ve got this ruddy book flying out of every bookshop in the known world!”\nMy face said what I had no words for.\n“Oh Christ, you idiot. What’s the repayment schedule?”\nI looked at my watch. “\003Three o’clock\004 this afternoon.”","Cloud Atlas","David Mitchell",false},
{"By \003three o’clock\004, the heat had morphed into a feral creature, a snake bent on prey. It hissed and slithered across the pavements, poked its flaming tongue through keyholes. People inched closer t their fans, sucked harder on ice cubes and opened the windows, only to close them instantly.","The Island of Missing Trees","Elif Shafak",true},
{"When the clock struck \003three\004, Elizabeth felt that she must go, and very unwillingly said so. Miss Bingley offered her the carriage, and she only wanted a little pressing to accept it, when Jane testified such concern in parting with her, that Miss Bingley was obliged to convert the offer of the chaise to an invitation to remain at Netherfield for the present.","Pride and Prejudice","Jane Austen",true},
{"I sat down on the woodpile we kept under a swatch of canvas on this side of the house. “I imagine you’re out here on business. My wife’s.”\n“I am.”\n“Well, you’ve had your drink, so we better get down to it. I’ve still got a full day’s work ahead of me, and it’s \003three o’clock in the afternoon\004.”","Full Dark, No Stars","Stephen King",true},
{"The storm swept through all of Europe, raging through the night and on into the next day, and when Cromwell died at \003three o’clock in the afternoon\004 it was still desolating the island.","Forever Amber","Kathleen Windsor",true},
{"At \003three o’clock precisely\004 I was at Baker Street, but Holmes had not yet returned.","A Scandal in Bohemia","Sir Arthur Conan Doyle",true},
{"And now it was \003three o’clock\004. The Antichrist had been on Earth for fifteen hours, and one angel and one demon had been drinking solidly for three of them.","Good Omens","Terry Pratchett and Neil Gaiman",true},
{"The city clocks had only just gone \003three\004, but it was quite dark already—it had not been light all day—and candles were flaring in the windows of the neighbouring offices, like ruddy smears upon the palpable brown air.","A Christmas Carol","Charles Dickens",true},
{"Dating from \003three o’clock\004 yesterday. My perpetual influence could not begin earlier, or you would not have been so much out of humour before.","Emma","Jane Austen",true},
{"Amory saw girls doing things that even in his memory would have been impossible: eating \003three-o’clock\004, after-dance suppers in impossible cafes, talking of every side of life with an air half of earnestness, half of mockery, yet with a furtive excitement that Amory considered stood for a real moral let-down. But he never realized how wide-spread it was until he saw the cities between New York and Chicago as one vast juvenile intrigue.","This Side of Paradise","F. Scott Fitzgerald",true},
{"The afternoon waned from the purging good of \003three o’clock\004 to the golden beauty of four.","This Side of Paradise","F. Scott Fitzgerald",true},
{"With some difficulty, James battled his way past waves of scent, screeching voices and glossy black hair.\n“What on earth is going on?” he asked when he reached the safety of his office.\nCarlo shrugged. “It’s \003three o’clock\004.”\n“I’m aware of the time, Carlo. Why are there so many women outside?”\n“They are the \001fidanzate\002. The women who want to marry Allied servicemen.”","The Wedding Officer","Anthony Capella",true},
{"I gotta get uptown by \003three o’clock\004.","A Confederacy of Dunces","John Kennedy Toole",true},
{"Remember, they shouted, “battle at \003three o’clock\004 sharp. There’s no time to lose.”","Swallows and Amazons","Arthur Ransome",true},
