{"At \003three o’clock\004 on the afternoon of that same day, he called on her. She held out her two hands, smiling in her usual charming, friendly way; and for a few seconds they looked deep into each other’s eyes.","Bel-Ami","Guy de Maupassant",true},
{"At \003three\004 on the Wednesday afternoon, that bit of the painting was completed.","The Moonstone","Wilkie Collins",true},
{"Ditched by the woman I loved, I exalted my suffering into a sign of greatness (lying collapsed on a bed at \003three\004 in the afternoon), and hence protected myself from experiencing my grief as the outcome of what was at best a mundane romantic break-up. Chloe’s departure may have killed me, but it had at least left me in glorious possession of the moral high ground. I was a martyr.","Essays in Love","Alain de Botton",true},
{"He walks into the Hospital for Broken Things at \003three o’clock\004 on Monday afternoon. That was the arrangement. If he came in after six o’clock, he was to head straight for the house in Sunset Park.","Sunset Park","Paul Auster",true},
{"I had a \003three o’clock\004 class in psychology, the first meeting of the semester, and I suspected I was going to miss it. I was right. Victoria made a real ritual of the whole thing, clothes coming off with the masturbatory dalliance of a strip show, the covers rolling back periodically to show this patch of flesh or that, strategically revealed.","Achates McNeil","T.C. Boyle",false},
{"It was \003three o’clock\004 in the beautiful breezy autumn day when Mr. Casaubon drove off to his Rectory at Lowick, only five miles from Tipton; and Dorothea, who had on her bonnet and shawl, hurried along the shrubbery and across the park that she might wander through the bordering wood with no other visible companionship than that of Monk, the Great St. Bernard dog, who always took care of the young ladies in their walks.","Middlemarch","George Eliot",true},
